This peer gives the subsystem domain an effective presentation, identifies its elements with subsystems, and characterizes which represented subsystems are effectively given.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`SubDX`).** The \(n\)-th basic neighbourhood of the effective subsystem domain is the neighbourhood generated by the finite list decoded from \(n\).

**Theorem (`SubDX_inter_eq`).** The basic neighbourhood indexed by the code for concatenating two decoded lists is the intersection of the two corresponding basic neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Concatenation of lists represents intersection of their generated neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`SubDX_masterIdx_spec`).** The code of the empty list indexes the master neighbourhood of the subsystem domain.

*Proof.* Decoding the encoded empty list returns the empty list. ∎

**Theorem (`SubDX_mem`).** Every indexed set is a neighbourhood of the subsystem domain.

*Proof.* It is generated by its decoded list. ∎

**Theorem (`SubDX_surj`).** Every neighbourhood of the subsystem domain has an index.

*Proof.* Encode a finite list that generates the given neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`SubD_cons_computable`).** It is decidable, uniformly in two indices, whether some indexed neighbourhood is contained in their intersection.

*Proof.* The code of the concatenated decoded lists always indexes exactly that intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`consUpd_foldl_idx`).** Iterating the consistency-state update along a list produces, in its index component, the successive intersection of the projected entries selected by the binary mask.

*Proof.* Induction on the list separates the selected and unselected head cases; only a selected head contributes the next intersection. ∎

**Definition (`idxCharAt`).** The characteristic index at a coded pair is the index component obtained by folding the consistency-state transition over the decoded list, starting from the master index.

**Theorem (`idxCharAt_eq_idxchain`).** The characteristic index equals the canonical index of the intersection generated by the entries selected from the decoded list.

*Proof.* The fold calculation identifies its index component with the successive intersection defining the canonical index. ∎

**Theorem (`primrec_idxCharAt`).** If the consistency characteristic is primitive recursive, then so is the characteristic-index function.

*Proof.* Primitive recursion is preserved by the coded fold, pairing, projection, and composition operations used in the definition. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_finGen_computable`).** Membership of an indexed ambient neighbourhood in the finite system generated by a decoded list is uniformly decidable.

*Proof.* Search the finitely many binary masks for a selected sublist whose intersection is an ambient neighbourhood and has the required canonical index; all constituent tests are decidable. ∎

**Theorem (`finGen_subset_computable`).** Inclusion between the finite systems generated by two decoded lists is uniformly decidable.

*Proof.* Such inclusion holds exactly when every generator in the first finite list belongs to the second finite system, reducing the question to a bounded finite universal test. ∎

**Theorem (`SubD_interEq_computable`).** Equality of the intersection of two indexed subsystem neighbourhoods with a third indexed neighbourhood is uniformly decidable.

*Proof.* Convert neighbourhood equality into equality of the associated finite generated systems, express equality as mutual inclusion, and use decidability of inclusion. ∎

**Definition (`SubDPresentation`).** The subsystem domain receives the computable presentation whose indexing decodes finite lists, whose intersection operation concatenates their codes, and whose master index is the code of the empty list.

**Definition (`subsystemIso`).** Elements of the subsystem domain and subsystems of the ambient domain are order-isomorphic.

**Theorem (`primrec_encodeList_singleton`).** Encoding a singleton list is a primitive-recursive function of its entry.

*Proof.* The singleton code is obtained from pairing with the empty-list code followed by successor. ∎

**Theorem (`subsystemU_computable_iff_index_set_re`).** An element of the effective subsystem domain is computable exactly when the set of indices of ambient basic neighbourhoods belonging to its represented subsystem is recursively enumerable.

*Proof.* Confirmation of a generated neighbourhood is equivalent to membership of all indices in its decoded finite list. Singleton coding gives one direction, and effective finite universal quantification over decoded lists gives the other. ∎

**Theorem (`subsystemU_element_computable_iff`).** An element of the effective subsystem domain is computable exactly when the basic-neighbourhood indices of its represented subsystem form the range of a primitive-recursive function.

*Proof.* The index set is nonempty because it contains the master index, so the standard range characterization of nonempty recursively enumerable predicates applies. ∎

**Theorem (`subsystemU_effectivelyGiven_of_isComputableElement`).** Every computable element of the universal subsystem domain represents an effectively given subsystem.

*Proof.* A primitive-recursive enumeration of its basic-neighbourhood index set supplies the required effective presentation. ∎

**Theorem (`subsystem_isEffectivelyGiven_of_isEffectivelyGiven`).** For every effectively given neighbourhood system there is an effectively given neighbourhood system whose elements are order-isomorphic to its subsystems.

*Proof.* Choose a computable presentation, equip its subsystem domain with the effective presentation above, and use the subsystem order isomorphism. ∎
